.SS Permissions
.PP
Reading \fI/proc/[pid]/maps\fR or \fI/proc/[pid]/smaps\fR is gated by
a \fBPTRACE_MODE_READ_FSCREDS\fR check (see \fBproc_pid_maps\fR(5)
and \fBptrace\fR(2)). For same-uid processes this typically succeeds.
Two cases will deny access:
.IP \(bu 2
The target's \fBdumpable\fR attribute is 0 — set when a process
execs a set-user-ID or set-group-ID binary. Files under /proc/[pid]/
become root-owned and only \fBCAP_SYS_PTRACE\fR can read the maps.
.IP \(bu 2
\fI/proc\fR is mounted with \fBhidepid=1\fR or \fBhidepid=2\fR (see
\fBproc\fR(5), common in container runtimes), which hides
/proc/[pid] entries owned by other users.
.SS Why \-x is more expensive
.PP
\fI/proc/[pid]/smaps\fR is computed lazily by walking each mapping's
page tables, so opening it for a large process can take noticeably
longer than \fI/proc/[pid]/maps\fR. On a process with thousands of
mappings, \fBpmap \-x\fR can pause the process briefly while the
kernel populates the file. \fBpmap\fR (default) only reads the
\fImaps\fR file, which is essentially free.
.SS Address space and rounding
.PP
Mappings are page-aligned by the kernel; the \fIKbytes\fR column is
\fI(end \- start)\fR / 1024 and is always a multiple of the page
size (typically 4 KiB on x86_64 / aarch64). Total virtual size is
shown in a footer line under the default and extended modes; with
\fB\-q\fR that footer is omitted.
